00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.410
that we need to carefully
and responsibly manage.

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.000
That data also
needs to be sifted

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.010
for whatever insights
we can glean in order

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:14.250
to better serve our customers.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.020
So we're a growing business.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.000
And we're moving into new
lines of business all the time.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.570
Some of those areas come
replete with their own data

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:23.070
specific challenges.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:26.340
In some instances,
we're confronted

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.860
with a particular
security strictness

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.300
that we have to contend with.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.890
In other areas we
have compliance

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.950
and regulatory regimes
that we have to deal with.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:37.110
In still other areas we
have particular data volume

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:40.660
and velocity challenges that
we need to contend with.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:44.010
So all of this is to say that
over the course of our 100 year

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.260
history and a number of
mergers and acquisitions

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:47.760
where every new
member of the family

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.370
has come with new data
standards, new data

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.280
processes, systems,
our landscape has

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.050
grown extremely complex.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:57.300
This complexity has been
very hard to contend with.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:01.150
It's hard just to grapple
with it, to understand it,

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:03.390
let alone to try
and merge it all,

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:07.490
fuse it into a unified whole.

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:09.470
So to try and
understand where some

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.990
of the places we were
falling short are,

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:14.360
we recently conducted a
survey of all the participants

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:16.670
in our data ecosystem
from which we

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:19.190
learned that the average
score people were

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:21.200
willing to give to
our data and our data

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.090
systems was a rather middling
2.74 on a five point scale.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.650
Not as good as it could
be, unfortunately.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.130
The data that we
received was rich enough

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:32.840
to reveal an interesting
pattern, which

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.543
was that the closer you
are to generating data,

00:02:35.543 --> 00:02:37.460
particularly if you're
a human that only deals

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:40.730
with a single database or a
single data generating process,

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:43.687
the happier you tended to
be with your lot in life.

00:02:43.687 --> 00:02:45.770
If you're a person that
was responsible for taking

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.137
data and transforming
it, somehow relocating

00:02:48.137 --> 00:02:50.720
it or handing it off, you were
generally a little less content

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.040
with your lot.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.960
And if you were a
downstream consumer of data,

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.550
you were generally
somewhat unhappy.

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:58.610
This is particularly
if you needed

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:00.950
to rely on a synthesis of
data, cross-functional data

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:03.433
from a lot of different
upstream data sources.

00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:04.850
So generally
speaking, the picture

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:07.392
was that the higher you went in
the organization and the more

00:03:07.392 --> 00:03:09.920
data you had to touch,
the more unhappy you were.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.260
And since that describes
senior level decision makers,

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:16.490
we realized that we had a bit of
a problem that we had to solve.

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:18.800
The implication for
us was that we needed

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:20.510
to walk before we could run.

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:22.010
We're very interested
in exploring

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.930
some sophisticated capabilities
like machine learning.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:26.690
We wanted to build
out an AI program.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:28.190
But we realized
that before we could

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:30.395
do any of that we needed
to fix our data problems.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:35.030
So the answer for
us was to explore

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:37.370
the principles of
supply chain management

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:41.060
and to undertake a data
supply chain program.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.010
So building a data
supply chain is

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:45.440
replete with many challenges.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.900
Those challenges include
the need to integrate data

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.300
from a great number of
sources, integrating

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:54.950
meaning clean, validate, refine,
reconcile data through an often

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:59.600
complex chain of
lengthy transformations.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:02.210
We also have to handle
the entire data lifecycle.

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:05.030
Our data is born, it lives
and is in active use,

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:07.775
and it retires and
eventually becomes obsolete.

00:04:07.775 --> 00:04:09.650
And during the entire
course of these events,

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:12.290
we need to avoid breakage.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:15.410
We need to provide a consistent
and canonical view of data

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:16.490
to our users.

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:20.240
And in particular, we want to
compute key business metrics

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:23.750
just once and in just
one way if possible.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:26.340
We needed to better
understand our data.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:29.720
We need an ontology to ascribe
shared meaning to data.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:32.090
And in particular we needed
comprehensive metadata

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:34.700
and lineage information
about all of our data

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:37.520
down to the field
level for every field.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.980
We wanted to build a
unitary system, something

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:43.695
that supported many roles
from less to more technical

00:04:43.695 --> 00:04:45.820
without causing an explosion
in the number of tools

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:47.470
that we had to build or support.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:49.720
And we wanted something that
was ubiquitous, something

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:51.137
that lived in all
the places where

00:04:51.137 --> 00:04:55.750
our data lived and made data
location transparency pivotal.

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:58.570
Ultimately, we needed safe and
secure delivery of our data

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:00.280
to our downstream consumers.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.650
We wanted to break out of
all the data silos that

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:05.910
existed in the enterprise.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.070
All of this has to happen
in the context of a world

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:10.830
where cloud is an important
part of what we do.

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:13.890
And we've learned that building
hybrid cloud architectures

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:15.060
is challenging.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:17.880
Some of those challenges include
the need for portability.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:20.730
We need to build things
locally, develop artifacts

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:23.850
that we can run locally but also
run on prem in our data lake

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:25.920
for example, as
well as to moving

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.900
into the cloud for
scaling and other benefits

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:29.580
that cloud offers.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:32.100
We need to be able to distribute
our data pipelines so they

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.530
seamlessly bridge
from on prem to cloud

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:38.640
so that our data can flow back
and forth without any friction.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:40.410
We need extensibility.

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:42.960
So we need standard
hooks, well-defined places

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.800
to add our own business logic,
to add data transformations,

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:48.630
to add connectors to
various data sources,

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.640
ideally without
bespoke integration.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.230
Every bit of
bespoke code that we

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:53.940
have to write to
support these things

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:56.490
means an additional burden
in terms of technical debt

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:59.200
that we'd rather avoid.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:00.990
We have the problem
with affinity.

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:04.050
Some applications just
resist hybridization.

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.750
Some data sets have to be
pinned to a certain location

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:08.310
for various reasons.

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:10.770
Conversely, some data
has to be duplicated.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:13.410
And that comes with its
attendant challenges

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:15.810
of having a unified
view of your data,

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:18.930
maintaining data
lineage, and so on.

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:21.300
And the problem of
testability is key.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:24.720
In as much as things vary from
environment to environment,

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.360
we want to isolate those
things, test them once.

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:29.960
We want to test once not
test once per environment.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:30.480
Right.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:32.063
We want sufficient
abstraction that we

00:06:32.063 --> 00:06:36.210
can build our pipelines, build
our data transformations,

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.160
build our logic and
test it once and know

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:42.410
that it's going to run in all
the places where we need it to.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:45.450
So taking GCP as our
example of a single cloud--

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:47.390
we're here, why not--

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:50.270
we realize that we suffer from
an embarrassment of riches.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:52.400
The great number of
services on offer

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:55.400
mean that we are faced with
service integration challenges.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:58.010
We're faced with a fairly
steep learning curve.

00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:00.290
Building even a relatively
simple pipeline--

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:04.310
imagine landing data on GCS,
modifying it with Dataproc,

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:06.830
pushing it into
BigQuery or Bigtable,

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:09.560
and ultimately surfacing
it in Data Studio--

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:11.810
recall what it was like to
be a newbie to the platform

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:13.310
and how daunting
all of those things

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:16.228
seemed, and then imagine having
to stitch them all together.

00:07:16.228 --> 00:07:18.020
This is something that
we needed to come up

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:19.550
with a way to address.

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:21.583
All of this impacts our
speed to productivity.

00:07:21.583 --> 00:07:23.000
The more things
that were touching

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:26.130
and trying to stitch together,
the greater our challenge.

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:28.670
We wanted to find a way
to make easy things easy.

00:07:28.670 --> 00:07:31.880
In spite of the fact that
digging into specific tools

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:35.040
yields benefits, greater
performance, lower cost,

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:37.190
we still need a way
to tie things together

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:38.570
without having to
be deep experts

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:42.910
in every single part of the
data pipelines that we build.

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:45.510
So multi-cloud.

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:48.742
We live in a multi-cloud world
and there is no escaping it.

00:07:48.742 --> 00:07:50.700
This comes with the
challenge of finding people

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:52.190
that are sufficiently skilled.

00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:55.350
Finding folks that are
knowledgeable about one cloud

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:56.820
platform is hard.

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:58.800
Finding unicorns
that know and have

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:02.250
deep expertise across multiple
clouds is correspondingly more

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:05.610
difficult. When things
inevitably go wrong,

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:07.290
finding the root
cause of the problems

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:08.850
is made much more
complicated when

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:12.690
your applications are parceled
out across multiple clouds.

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:14.670
And with a greater mix
of tools and services

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:19.140
available across clouds,
finding the right mix of things

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:23.070
to tailor for precisely your
application is more difficult.

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:24.660
And of course, there
may come a time

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:27.757
when you want to move on to
or off of a particular cloud.

00:08:27.757 --> 00:08:30.090
And to the extent that you've
tailored your applications

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:31.920
to the interfaces
of those cloud,

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:36.270
you'll find that you have a
higher switching cost to pay.

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:39.500
So the question before us is,
can we find or build a tool

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:43.400
to tie a broad array of services
together near seamlessly

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:45.650
across our on prem and
cloud infrastructure,

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:47.760
multi-cloud
infrastructure in fact,

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.390
in a way that minimizes the
cognitive load of taking

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:52.640
advantage of all this power?

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:54.380
We want a platform
that supports the end

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:57.770
to end data lifecycle, something
that helps us manage data

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:02.493
from the moment of its capture
till its eventual retirement.

00:09:02.493 --> 00:09:04.910
We want something that helps
us better understand our data

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:08.030
and processes, something
that explains the origin

00:09:08.030 --> 00:09:12.320
and the lineage of all the data
that flows through the system.

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:15.440
We want something that
supports a broad mix of less

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:17.270
technical and more
technical users,

00:09:17.270 --> 00:09:20.240
giving all of these folks a
home, something within which

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:23.090
they can easily access the
data that they need and build

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:26.290
flows to shape it in
the way that they want.

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:28.230
And to add on a few
extra bullet points,

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:30.188
if we could find something
that was open source

00:09:30.188 --> 00:09:32.120
and that has a community,
so much the better.

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:35.360
If we can find something that
we know scales from micro jobs

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:37.940
all the way up to massive,
and if we could find something

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:40.357
that's sufficiently flexible
that we can mold and shape it

00:09:40.357 --> 00:09:43.430
to our needs, we'd
be that much happier.

00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:45.680
So about a year ago
I was introduced

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:47.390
to something called CDAP.

00:09:47.390 --> 00:09:51.170
CDAP was an open source tool
for big data processing.

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:53.890
And having dug into it,
I became very excited.

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:55.640
It seemed to address
a lot of these points

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:57.230
that I've mentioned.

00:09:57.230 --> 00:09:59.640
And it wasn't so long after
first encountering the tool

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.600
and getting excited I learned
that it had joined the Google

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:05.060
Cloud family of
services and had become

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:08.000
what you're going to hear
about today, Data Fusion.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:10.880
Since then, Data Fusion has
become an important part

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:14.750
of our data integration efforts
as we move our data supply

00:10:14.750 --> 00:10:17.780
chain into a hybrid
and multi-cloud world.

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:20.090
And Data Fusion solves
a number of the pain

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:21.260
points that we suffer.

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:23.810
And I'd like to introduce Nitin
to tell you a little bit more

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:26.540
about it.

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:27.530
NITIN MOTGI: Thank you.

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:28.210
Thank you.

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:28.960
Thank you, Robert.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:30.085
ROBERT MEDEIROS: Thank you.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:35.050
NITIN MOTGI: So let's
talk about Data Fusion.

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:36.220
So Data Fusion.

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:37.960
What is Data Fusion?

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:41.050
So Data Fusion is a fully
managed cloud native data

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:42.910
integration service.

00:10:42.910 --> 00:10:46.360
It's basically helping you to
efficiently build and manage

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:47.800
data pipelines.

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:50.110
With a graphical interface,
and a broad collection

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:52.660
of open source
transformations, and hundreds

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:56.890
of out of box connectors,
it helps organizations

00:10:56.890 --> 00:11:00.520
shift the focus from
code and integration

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:02.950
to insights and actions.

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:09.640
It's actually built on an open
source technology called CDAP.

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:13.250
CDAP has been in existence
for quite some time now.

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:15.220
This is a managed version of it.

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:17.520
So we can talk more
about CDAP in general.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:20.740
But CDAP as a platform
allows you to build data

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:22.430
analytical applications.

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:24.670
And as part of data
analytical applications,

00:11:24.670 --> 00:11:27.400
there are a few analytical
applications that are also

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:29.110
included as part of CDAP.

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:32.740
And one of them is ability
to build data pipelines.

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:35.110
The second one is
more about how do

00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:38.380
you transform the data without
having to write any code.

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:40.090
We call them
Wrangler, data prep.

00:11:40.090 --> 00:11:42.710
The name has been
constantly changing.

00:11:42.710 --> 00:11:45.970
But it's an ability where if you
are specifying transformations,

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:47.470
you're applying
data quality checks,

00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:49.460
you don't need to
be writing any code.

00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:51.940
And that is another accelerative
[INAUDIBLE] application

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:53.750
that is built on top of CDAP.

00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:55.930
So we basically-- there
are more other things

00:11:55.930 --> 00:11:57.430
like that like rules
engine, a bunch

00:11:57.430 --> 00:12:00.660
of things like that, which
we will look at it later.

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:03.400
But what we have done now
is taken those two parts

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:05.860
with ability to build
pipelines and add ability

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:08.560
to do transformations
without having to write code.

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:10.660
And along with
the CDAP platform,

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:14.920
we have turned that into
a managed offering on GCP.

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:17.680
So when you look at
the kinds of use cases

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:20.190
that we can use to solve
this, and the problems that it

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:22.330
is trying to address, first
thing that it is trying

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:24.010
to address, like
Robert mentioned,

00:12:24.010 --> 00:12:28.010
it's making it extremely
easy to move data around.

00:12:28.010 --> 00:12:28.510
Right.

00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:31.640
So right now, if you're
doing it all by yourself,

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:33.040
it's very error prone.

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:34.280
It's very time consuming.

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:39.082
So if I have to move data from,
let's say GCS to BigQuery,

00:12:39.082 --> 00:12:40.540
I would have to
spend a lot of time

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:42.760
writing code and ensuring
that that code actually

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:44.710
works every single time.

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:47.680
That is not to say if I have
to do another such point

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:50.560
A to point B transition
with added transformation,

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:52.220
I start all over again.

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:56.000
So essentially, at the end
of it, it increases your TCO.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:56.500
Right.

00:12:56.500 --> 00:13:00.100
So just for moving, you're
spending way too much time

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:03.863
and then you're actually
getting into situations

00:13:03.863 --> 00:13:06.280
where you might not be able
to address all of the business

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:07.540
requirements that you have.

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:08.770
Right.

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:11.240
And also skills gap
is another thing.

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:13.750
So it's like you need to
have right set of skills

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:16.167
and have good expertise
in the systems

00:13:16.167 --> 00:13:18.250
that you're stitching them
together, because there

00:13:18.250 --> 00:13:19.458
are performance requirements.

00:13:19.458 --> 00:13:21.850
There a bunch of things
related to how-tos, best

00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:24.430
practices you need
to be adhering to.

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:26.950
So all that is very
difficult. Right.

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:29.050
And the last one is hybrid.

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:33.910
And hybrid is something that
is very interesting actually.

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:36.670
During our process through
EAP, which is the early access

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:42.520
program on GCP, we learned that
actual journey to cloud starts

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:43.420
on prem.

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:45.910
It's not like lift and shift
and everything is done.

00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:46.990
It's kind of hard.

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:50.320
You need to be able to
start your journey slowly

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:52.365
from an on prem environment.

00:13:52.365 --> 00:13:54.490
So just by the combination
of CDAP and Data Fusion,

00:13:54.490 --> 00:13:56.530
it allows you to do so.

00:13:56.530 --> 00:13:58.265
And you don't need
to build it twice.

00:13:58.265 --> 00:14:00.140
You can build it once
and be able to run them

00:14:00.140 --> 00:14:03.330
in two different environments.

00:14:03.330 --> 00:14:06.700
So who are the users that we
are targeting with Data Fusion?

00:14:06.700 --> 00:14:07.860
So we are leveling--

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:09.690
we are moving the
level up in terms

00:14:09.690 --> 00:14:12.810
of being able to
adopt GCP and being

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:14.580
able to apply transformations.

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:15.860
When you look at--

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:18.330
when there is a need for
developer data scientist

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:22.710
or business analyst to cleanse,
transform, blend, transfer

00:14:22.710 --> 00:14:26.190
data, or standardize,
canonicalize, automate

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:29.190
your data operations,
data ops, we

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:32.910
want you to be
using Data Fusion.

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:35.010
Data Fusion provides
a graphical interface.

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:37.200
With that, it also
provides ability for you

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:42.040
to test, debug,
as well as deploy.

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:45.640
You can deploy it
at scale on GCP.

00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:48.540
And there we can essentially
scale to your data levels.

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:49.040
Right.

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:54.190
So it essentially can scale
to petabytes of data there.

00:14:54.190 --> 00:14:58.630
Now in addition to that, we
also collect a lot of metadata,

00:14:58.630 --> 00:15:02.590
whether it is business,
technical, or operational.

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:07.490
All of this metadata is
aggregated within Data Fusion.

00:15:07.490 --> 00:15:11.360
And we are exposing that
in terms of lineage.

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:13.780
So you will be able to
do things like root cause

00:15:13.780 --> 00:15:21.090
analysis, impact analysis,
be able to find provenance,

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:24.630
be able to associate a lot of
metadata for the data pipelines

00:15:24.630 --> 00:15:26.370
that you're building
as well as data sets

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:28.180
that you're creating with it.

00:15:28.180 --> 00:15:32.570
And that's a very important
part of having data operations

00:15:32.570 --> 00:15:33.780
anywhere.

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:37.200
So when you look at
the kinds of use cases

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:43.363
that Data Fusion is trying
to solve, Data Fusion--

00:15:43.363 --> 00:15:45.030
these are different
business imperatives

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:46.830
that translate
into IT initiatives

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:50.410
and then maps to how Data
Fusion can help solve.

00:15:50.410 --> 00:15:52.380
So when you are looking
to build warehouses,

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:53.797
you're actually
trying to get data

00:15:53.797 --> 00:15:56.780
from many different sources,
let's say into BigQuery

00:15:56.780 --> 00:15:59.535
as an example.

00:15:59.535 --> 00:16:01.410
You should make it
extremely easy to do that.

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:04.438
It should not take you years,
six months, not that long.

00:16:04.438 --> 00:16:06.480
You should be able to
operationalize these things

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:08.400
pretty quickly.

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:13.640
When you are actually looking
to retire legacy systems,

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:18.380
Data Fusion can help you
migrate that data over to GCP.

00:16:18.380 --> 00:16:19.880
it doesn't need to
be just BigQuery.

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:21.020
You can put it in Spanner.

00:16:21.020 --> 00:16:24.800
You can put it in Cloud
SQL, data store, Bigtable.

00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:26.840
You can pretty much
be able to connect

00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:30.650
to anything that is available
on GCP to bring that data in.

00:16:30.650 --> 00:16:32.860
And it's not just limited
from a source's perspective

00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:33.530
to on prem.

00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:35.750
You can also read from
the same set of sources

00:16:35.750 --> 00:16:38.750
that you have written to in GCP.

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:40.610
Data consolidation
is another aspect.

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:42.680
So you're trying to
either migrate some data

00:16:42.680 --> 00:16:47.432
or essentially retire a bunch
of data that exists there.

00:16:47.432 --> 00:16:48.890
Master data management
is something

00:16:48.890 --> 00:16:51.750
that has a bunch of
capabilities but we

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:55.340
are going to be adding
a lot more in this year

00:16:55.340 --> 00:16:57.740
to essentially help
create a much more

00:16:57.740 --> 00:17:01.880
consistent, high quality
environment for your data.

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:03.850
And the last one is
extend or migrate

00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:05.780
in cases on a hybrid
environment that you

00:17:05.780 --> 00:17:08.023
want to load shed into cloud.

00:17:08.023 --> 00:17:09.440
If you are still
on prem, you will

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:11.665
be able to do those
kind of things.

00:17:11.665 --> 00:17:13.040
The thing is the
complexity of it

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:17.940
being able to run on cloud,
on prem, and in other clouds.

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:20.300
That's the biggest
part of all this.

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:24.770
You should be able to
run that across anywhere.

00:17:24.770 --> 00:17:27.890
So just to put it all
together, so the way

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:32.630
I like to think about
this is Data Fusion

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:36.500
is providing a
fabric which allows

00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:40.610
you to fuse a lot of different
technologies and products

00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:46.250
that are available on GCP in
a much more easy, accessible,

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:49.980
secure, performant and
an intelligent manner.

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:53.770
It just makes the entire
process extremely easy.

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:56.200
So the things that would
take me six weeks to build,

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:58.030
I can literally build
them in two minutes,

00:17:58.030 --> 00:18:01.215
deploy them on the third minute,
and be able to operationalize.

00:18:01.215 --> 00:18:02.840
Operationalize should
not be that easy,

00:18:02.840 --> 00:18:06.280
but like it takes
let's say a day or so.

00:18:06.280 --> 00:18:07.180
Right.

00:18:07.180 --> 00:18:10.180
So basically, this is bringing
a lot of different things

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:12.960
together, making it very simple.

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:18.550
It's moving up the bar,
lowering the barrier to entry

00:18:18.550 --> 00:18:23.420
essentially for GCP when
you talk in terms of data.

00:18:23.420 --> 00:18:26.380
So with that brief
introduction, I'm

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:29.410
going to show you guys
a demo of Data Fusion.

00:18:41.170 --> 00:18:43.280
So let's get started.

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:46.720
So Data Fusion, as I said,
is a managed offering.

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:49.840
So it will be available
through Cloud console.

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:52.810
So you will be,
starting today, you

00:18:52.810 --> 00:18:57.730
will be able to see Data Fusion
show up on the Cloud console.

00:18:57.730 --> 00:19:00.850
So now what we have done
is we have basically

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:04.900
taken Data Fusion and given
two different editions-- we

00:19:04.900 --> 00:19:09.190
have made two different
editions available to you all.

00:19:09.190 --> 00:19:10.853
One is the basic edition.

00:19:10.853 --> 00:19:12.520
The second one is an
enterprise edition.

00:19:12.520 --> 00:19:15.760
You should be able to pick
depending on your needs.

00:19:15.760 --> 00:19:17.860
And we recommend
basic edition to be

00:19:17.860 --> 00:19:22.690
more for dev I would say, not
so highly available to your QA

00:19:22.690 --> 00:19:23.380
environment.

00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:26.860
But enterprise edition is more
for mission critical stuff.

00:19:26.860 --> 00:19:29.380
So you should be able to
provision off of those two

00:19:29.380 --> 00:19:31.550
editions to start with.

00:19:31.550 --> 00:19:34.713
So here I have already
created a few of them.

00:19:34.713 --> 00:19:36.130
I mean, these are
all you can see.

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:39.160
These are Next demo stuff, so
a bunch of different things

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:41.500
that have been created
and all in enterprise.

00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:43.870
But I can go into
Create Instance

00:19:43.870 --> 00:19:46.690
and be able to specify.

00:19:46.690 --> 00:19:49.657
And here are the
zones and locations

00:19:49.657 --> 00:19:50.740
that we are starting with.

00:19:50.740 --> 00:19:55.570
So we have Asia East,
Europe West, Central, East,

00:19:55.570 --> 00:19:56.990
and West, all of these.

00:19:56.990 --> 00:20:00.090
So as we go along, we'll
be adding more locations

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:01.420
in this year.

00:20:03.930 --> 00:20:08.940
So once I clear the instance,
I can jump into Data Fusion

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:09.600
entirely.

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:10.950
So this is Data Fusion.

00:20:10.950 --> 00:20:15.120
It looks a lot like a
cockpit where essentially you

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:17.807
have a lot of things that
you have interacted with.

00:20:17.807 --> 00:20:19.140
This is called a control center.

00:20:19.140 --> 00:20:23.580
Control center is the place
where you are actually

00:20:23.580 --> 00:20:28.020
able to monitor and be able to
manage all of your data sets

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:30.490
and data pipelines.

00:20:30.490 --> 00:20:33.330
This is a central
place for doing that.

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:39.350
So you also have an ability
to work with the pipelines.

00:20:39.350 --> 00:20:41.420
This is the list of
explicit pipelines

00:20:41.420 --> 00:20:42.800
that you are interested in here.

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:45.140
We have put a few pipelines
that have been scheduled

00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:47.870
to run and have been running.

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:51.530
Additionally, you
also have a studio

00:20:51.530 --> 00:20:54.692
where you can build
different pipelines.

00:20:54.692 --> 00:20:56.900
It's a visual way of building
all of these pipelines,

00:20:56.900 --> 00:20:59.130
being able to deploy them.

00:20:59.130 --> 00:21:05.500
And also, we have a place where
we are collecting metadata.

00:21:05.500 --> 00:21:06.760
I skipped on one, Wrangler.

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:08.460
I'll come back to that.

00:21:08.460 --> 00:21:10.960
This is where we are collecting
all of the pipeline metadata

00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:12.710
as well as all of the
different data sets.

00:21:12.710 --> 00:21:15.210
The data gets collected here.

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:16.560
So the interesting part.

00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:19.610
Now let's imagine you have data
sitting in an Oracle system.

00:21:19.610 --> 00:21:21.260
So like it's an on
prem Oracle system

00:21:21.260 --> 00:21:22.510
and you want to bring data in.

00:21:22.510 --> 00:21:25.470
And it's not just limited
to Oracle actually.

00:21:25.470 --> 00:21:27.870
We have hundreds of
out of box connectors

00:21:27.870 --> 00:21:30.000
that allow you to connect
to various systems

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:33.180
starting from mainframe
to being able to connect

00:21:33.180 --> 00:21:35.200
to a bunch of different
cloud sources.

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:36.300
Right.

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:39.450
So in order to
start with, we have

00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:43.170
started providing a very
simple way of bringing data in.

00:21:43.170 --> 00:21:44.010
Right.

00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:45.720
So first thing
you're seeing here

00:21:45.720 --> 00:21:48.563
is I have pulled in the
data from an Oracle table.

00:21:48.563 --> 00:21:49.980
Now let's see how
did we get here.

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:50.850
Right.

00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:55.460
So Wrangler allows
you to connect

00:21:55.460 --> 00:21:57.205
to various different
data sources.

00:21:57.205 --> 00:21:58.330
These are the popular ones.

00:21:58.330 --> 00:22:02.620
We are going to be adding a
lot more in in coming months

00:22:02.620 --> 00:22:03.700
actually.

00:22:03.700 --> 00:22:06.460
So I have already preconfigured
an Oracle table here.

00:22:06.460 --> 00:22:09.280
I can actually add
more databases.

00:22:09.280 --> 00:22:12.996
Databases that can support
JDBC can be easily added here.

00:22:12.996 --> 00:22:16.660
We also support Kafka, S3,
GCS, BigQuery, and stuff.

00:22:16.660 --> 00:22:18.910
So as soon as your
instance gets created,

00:22:18.910 --> 00:22:21.130
it automatically
attaches to your projects

00:22:21.130 --> 00:22:24.100
in GCS, BigQuery, and Spanner.

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:27.240
So now let's say I have
data sitting in Oracle here.

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:31.250
So this is now live listing
all of the Oracle tables.

00:22:31.250 --> 00:22:34.370
And I'm interested in
bringing in one table

00:22:34.370 --> 00:22:37.040
only, not to say that
you have ability to also

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:39.347
bring the entire database over.

00:22:39.347 --> 00:22:41.180
So you don't have to
do one table at a time.

00:22:41.180 --> 00:22:43.490
You can bring the
entire database over.

00:22:43.490 --> 00:22:46.220
So let me click on
the employees table.

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:48.900
As soon as I click,
the data gets sampled.

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:51.260
There are many different
ways the data can be sampled.

00:22:51.260 --> 00:22:52.970
But the data gets sampled.

00:22:52.970 --> 00:22:56.750
All of the data types of
the source data system

00:22:56.750 --> 00:22:59.090
gets translated into
an intermediate, which

00:22:59.090 --> 00:23:01.370
is essentially it's
AVRO, where it gets

00:23:01.370 --> 00:23:03.410
translated into AVRO types.

00:23:03.410 --> 00:23:06.360
So it's basically an AVRO spec,
but it is an extended AVRO spec

00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:08.880
to specify various other types.

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:11.850
So now once I'm here, I
can apply transformations.

00:23:11.850 --> 00:23:14.450
So now the first thing I see
is commission percentages

00:23:14.450 --> 00:23:16.760
being highlighted for like
there's some data that's

00:23:16.760 --> 00:23:18.110
actually missing here.

00:23:18.110 --> 00:23:20.240
Now in order to process,
I want to make sure

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:23.170
that I can specify
some default values.

00:23:23.170 --> 00:23:26.900
So I'm going to fill 0.0 here.

00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:30.040
So there's 0% commission.

00:23:30.040 --> 00:23:31.980
I can now see it's
100% complete.

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:33.040
Right.

00:23:33.040 --> 00:23:36.880
I can also see that the job ID
is a combination of two things,

00:23:36.880 --> 00:23:38.410
department and title.

00:23:38.410 --> 00:23:41.290
So I want to be able to
specify a way to split those.

00:23:41.290 --> 00:23:43.550
So I can go in here.

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:45.700
I say extract fields.

00:23:45.700 --> 00:23:49.560
Like delimiters.

00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:51.860
So now we generated two columns.

00:23:51.860 --> 00:23:52.620
Right.

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:57.340
So now I don't need this
specific column from here.

00:23:57.340 --> 00:23:58.330
And I'm good.

00:23:58.330 --> 00:23:59.088
Right.

00:23:59.088 --> 00:24:00.880
But you want to get a
little bit of insight

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:03.230
of how your data is organized.

00:24:03.230 --> 00:24:07.510
So we basically make it easy
to get a little bit of insights

00:24:07.510 --> 00:24:08.603
on your data.

00:24:08.603 --> 00:24:11.020
So there is a way where you
can essentially slice and dice

00:24:11.020 --> 00:24:14.110
this data in and have
a visual way of looking

00:24:14.110 --> 00:24:16.140
at how the different
dimensions are organized,

00:24:16.140 --> 00:24:18.820
how many different
records of a certain type.

00:24:18.820 --> 00:24:20.820
So you can do
drill down on these

00:24:20.820 --> 00:24:24.430
and look at and compare that
with other dimensions, right?

00:24:24.430 --> 00:24:27.700
So this is just to give you a
feel of how your data that you

00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:29.260
have pulled in is--

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:31.140
how the sample that
you have been pulled--

00:24:31.140 --> 00:24:33.270
is organized.

00:24:33.270 --> 00:24:35.710
So once you are
comfortable with this,

00:24:35.710 --> 00:24:38.355
I will say go create a pipeline
because my source right now

00:24:38.355 --> 00:24:38.855
is Oracle.

00:24:41.882 --> 00:24:43.560
It's not a real
time source, right?

00:24:43.560 --> 00:24:45.935
So it's a bad source right
now, because we are pulling it

00:24:45.935 --> 00:24:48.230
in bad, but you can
also do the real time

00:24:48.230 --> 00:24:50.900
data pull from Oracle, so
if you are doing things

00:24:50.900 --> 00:24:53.290
like CDC and such.

00:24:53.290 --> 00:24:54.950
So I want to create
a batch pipeline.

00:24:54.950 --> 00:24:57.550
So what that does is, it
transfers all the work that I

00:24:57.550 --> 00:25:05.140
did in Wrangler into my
studio, where I can upload

00:25:05.140 --> 00:25:06.310
the rest of the pipeline.

00:25:06.310 --> 00:25:08.230
My goal was to get
this transformation.

00:25:08.230 --> 00:25:10.720
Now, I started with
Wrangler to create this,

00:25:10.720 --> 00:25:12.320
but you can go the
other way, too.

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:16.060
You can actually go from being
in this canvas sort of Studio

00:25:16.060 --> 00:25:17.410
to being able to wrangle that.

00:25:17.410 --> 00:25:20.140
So it's just by dropping
one of the transforms,

00:25:20.140 --> 00:25:22.120
like Wrangler transforms
here, you should

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:24.680
be able to do that seamlessly.

00:25:24.680 --> 00:25:27.360
For simplicity I'm just
using a very simple pipeline.

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:29.270
So if you then you
want to be able to,

00:25:29.270 --> 00:25:32.270
let's say, now this is
not operable-- this is not

00:25:32.270 --> 00:25:34.580
going to be operating
on just a sample,

00:25:34.580 --> 00:25:36.680
it's going to be operating
on the entire table

00:25:36.680 --> 00:25:38.990
that you're trying to pull.

00:25:38.990 --> 00:25:41.450
So then I can go
ahead and figure out,

00:25:41.450 --> 00:25:46.310
like I would need to put this
into a BigQuery for analytics.

00:25:46.310 --> 00:25:49.670
I also want to make sure that
I can actually store this data

00:25:49.670 --> 00:25:53.440
or archive this data into GCS.

00:25:56.420 --> 00:25:59.760
Right, so it's as easy as that.

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:01.920
Now I configure these.

00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:03.960
Configurations are
also pretty easy here.

00:26:03.960 --> 00:26:06.850
So you just define
the configuration,

00:26:06.850 --> 00:26:09.480
which is your data set,
your table name, that's

00:26:09.480 --> 00:26:10.530
all you need to specify.

00:26:10.530 --> 00:26:13.170
Those are the mandatory
fields, you're done.

00:26:13.170 --> 00:26:17.180
That's how you essentially
bring data into BigQuery.

00:26:17.180 --> 00:26:20.360
The same holds true
for GCS perspective.

00:26:20.360 --> 00:26:22.280
You specify how
your bucket needs

00:26:22.280 --> 00:26:24.230
to be there, what do
you Suffix Path is,

00:26:24.230 --> 00:26:27.110
and you can also define
the format in which you

00:26:27.110 --> 00:26:31.030
want to write that data in.

00:26:31.030 --> 00:26:32.660
It's not limited to it.

00:26:32.660 --> 00:26:35.590
So now the Wrangler
that I specified,

00:26:35.590 --> 00:26:38.530
that I showed you guys,
that have more than 1,000+

00:26:38.530 --> 00:26:41.950
functions, which include a lot
of data quality checks which

00:26:41.950 --> 00:26:43.270
I'm going to show you guys.

00:26:43.270 --> 00:26:47.560
But the most important thing
with that is it is extendable,

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:50.590
you can write your own
data transformation--

00:26:50.590 --> 00:26:51.700
we call them directives.

00:26:51.700 --> 00:26:54.850
You can write your own data
transformation directives.

00:26:54.850 --> 00:26:59.200
And you can just drop them in
and use them in any pipeline.

00:26:59.200 --> 00:27:01.390
That gives you share-ability.

00:27:01.390 --> 00:27:05.500
So to show that, let me see,
after doing this I feel like,

00:27:05.500 --> 00:27:07.370
oh man, I missed something.

00:27:07.370 --> 00:27:11.460
So I want to go back and
do more operations here.

00:27:11.460 --> 00:27:15.600
Right, so I want to be able
to say, like, for example,

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:18.930
this Employee ID change my
type to, let's say, long.

00:27:18.930 --> 00:27:21.010
So I should be able to
do those type of things.

00:27:21.010 --> 00:27:22.980
So I go back and I make
those transformations

00:27:22.980 --> 00:27:24.400
and I am done with it.

00:27:27.140 --> 00:27:28.920
So, let's see, I go back.

00:27:28.920 --> 00:27:31.780
OK so all of the transformations
are here, so now whatever

00:27:31.780 --> 00:27:34.240
I have done can be configured.

00:27:34.240 --> 00:27:36.557
So right now the pipeline--

00:27:36.557 --> 00:27:38.140
we provide two
different ways of being

00:27:38.140 --> 00:27:39.603
able to execute the pipeline.

00:27:39.603 --> 00:27:42.270
You can run them as a Spark job,
you can run them as a MapReduce

00:27:42.270 --> 00:27:45.040
job, but we are also going
to be in the future adding

00:27:45.040 --> 00:27:48.520
an ability for you to run
that as a Dataflow job.

00:27:48.520 --> 00:27:50.740
So you build a
pipeline, you'll be

00:27:50.740 --> 00:27:52.630
able to run in any
of these three things

00:27:52.630 --> 00:27:58.240
without having to make any
change to your business logic.

00:27:58.240 --> 00:27:59.410
Let that sink in for a bit.

00:28:02.890 --> 00:28:05.890
You then have the ability
to specify resources.

00:28:05.890 --> 00:28:08.920
So, like, this is actually
for MapReduce, OK,

00:28:08.920 --> 00:28:10.910
that's not that interesting,
it's the old one.

00:28:10.910 --> 00:28:12.140
So let's go look at Spark.

00:28:12.140 --> 00:28:14.360
So you can change
how Spark behaves.

00:28:14.360 --> 00:28:16.060
So you can specify
configurations,

00:28:16.060 --> 00:28:17.680
you can specify
a very specific--

00:28:17.680 --> 00:28:19.852
We don't recommend,
actually, for you to do this,

00:28:19.852 --> 00:28:21.310
I'm just showing
you that these are

00:28:21.310 --> 00:28:24.610
available if you guys are
interested in fine tuning

00:28:24.610 --> 00:28:26.050
your particular pipeline.

00:28:26.050 --> 00:28:28.150
We also do support alerts.

00:28:28.150 --> 00:28:30.970
So again, these alerts--

00:28:30.970 --> 00:28:32.590
we do provide a
developer's ticket

00:28:32.590 --> 00:28:35.510
that allows you to extend
these and add your own thing.

00:28:35.510 --> 00:28:37.630
For example, one of
our users actually

00:28:37.630 --> 00:28:40.210
wrote a Slack connector,
so every time a pipeline

00:28:40.210 --> 00:28:42.670
finishes it sends
a Slack message.

00:28:42.670 --> 00:28:45.050
I think there was one--
someone wrote a HipChat one

00:28:45.050 --> 00:28:46.130
and there was Trilio one.

00:28:46.130 --> 00:28:47.720
Like a bunch of
things like that.

00:28:47.720 --> 00:28:49.990
So you can make
the notifications

00:28:49.990 --> 00:28:53.040
pretty easy with these.

00:28:53.040 --> 00:28:55.410
So, in addition
to this, you also

00:28:55.410 --> 00:28:58.290
have the ability to schedule
your pipelines from here.

00:28:58.290 --> 00:29:00.300
You can define them here
or you can define them

00:29:00.300 --> 00:29:02.350
once they're deployed.

00:29:02.350 --> 00:29:05.740
Now the interesting thing
here is you can actually

00:29:05.740 --> 00:29:06.700
debug the pipeline.

00:29:06.700 --> 00:29:09.430
As soon as you go
into the preview mode

00:29:09.430 --> 00:29:12.250
it provides you an ability
to test your pipeline.

00:29:12.250 --> 00:29:15.010
And it actually is going to
go to the original source

00:29:15.010 --> 00:29:16.780
and bring that data in.

00:29:16.780 --> 00:29:19.330
So right now it
supports N number.

00:29:19.330 --> 00:29:21.640
N is defaulted to
100 rows, but if you

00:29:21.640 --> 00:29:24.130
want to run a larger
sample, you should

00:29:24.130 --> 00:29:26.200
be able to do that, it
just takes a little bit

00:29:26.200 --> 00:29:29.050
more time when you are looking
to preview terabytes of data.

00:29:29.050 --> 00:29:31.360
But we are looking to
improve that and essentially

00:29:31.360 --> 00:29:33.430
be able to do this
constantly in real time

00:29:33.430 --> 00:29:35.130
so that you can look
at the data as you

00:29:35.130 --> 00:29:38.840
are building the pipeline.

00:29:38.840 --> 00:29:41.930
So all of this
actually translates--

00:29:41.930 --> 00:29:43.880
one question that
I always get asked

00:29:43.880 --> 00:29:48.290
is, Is this generating a
Spark or MapReduce code.

00:29:48.290 --> 00:29:49.580
Is it a code generator?

00:29:49.580 --> 00:29:53.465
And my answer to that is no,
it's not a code generator.

00:29:53.465 --> 00:29:55.090
I don't know how many
of you guys know,

00:29:55.090 --> 00:29:57.800
but the word that was
coined a long time ago

00:29:57.800 --> 00:29:59.640
called code weaving.

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:02.060
So it weaves all of the
different components

00:30:02.060 --> 00:30:03.920
that are being built
for one execution

00:30:03.920 --> 00:30:05.840
engine in an optimized way.

00:30:05.840 --> 00:30:08.000
So there is actually
a planner that

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:11.030
picks up all the bits and
pieces and translates that

00:30:11.030 --> 00:30:12.350
into an execution plan.

00:30:12.350 --> 00:30:16.730
So it weaves all of the stuff
that you'll build into a pack

00:30:16.730 --> 00:30:19.290
and it figures out what is the
optimal way of transferring

00:30:19.290 --> 00:30:21.020
the data from one
node to the other.

00:30:21.020 --> 00:30:24.320
In most cases it's amazing
because you're now going

00:30:24.320 --> 00:30:25.730
from one machine to the other.

00:30:25.730 --> 00:30:29.420
In most cases, you're actually
doing in memory transfers

00:30:29.420 --> 00:30:32.900
and that's how it gets composed
into the execution paradigm.

00:30:32.900 --> 00:30:37.190
And that's the same thing we
can do for Renegade and Spark,

00:30:37.190 --> 00:30:40.220
we can do the same thing
for Renegade and MapReduce.

00:30:40.220 --> 00:30:41.780
And with that,
the ability for us

00:30:41.780 --> 00:30:45.080
to be able to push down some
of those optimizations--

00:30:45.080 --> 00:30:46.910
basically leverage
the optimizations

00:30:46.910 --> 00:30:49.820
that are available in
the underlying system--

00:30:49.820 --> 00:30:51.130
is extremely critical.

00:30:51.130 --> 00:30:53.120
And we're able to do
that without having

00:30:53.120 --> 00:30:56.720
to make major architectural
changes to this.

00:30:56.720 --> 00:31:01.490
Now once a pipeline is built,
you can actually see it

00:31:01.490 --> 00:31:03.500
generates a
configuration, right?

00:31:03.500 --> 00:31:05.270
It's a simple JSON
configuration,

00:31:05.270 --> 00:31:07.830
you can build it all
by yourself with hand.

00:31:07.830 --> 00:31:09.060
It's not very complicated.

00:31:09.060 --> 00:31:11.060
It has just a few sections.

00:31:11.060 --> 00:31:14.750
And for everything
there, in addition

00:31:14.750 --> 00:31:17.240
to being able to
define the graphs,

00:31:17.240 --> 00:31:20.330
it actually tracks
every individual

00:31:20.330 --> 00:31:23.390
node the artifact version.

00:31:23.390 --> 00:31:25.490
So you can essentially
track every component

00:31:25.490 --> 00:31:28.500
that is being used, is
being versioned, actually.

00:31:28.500 --> 00:31:30.560
So you're able to
version every part of it.

00:31:30.560 --> 00:31:34.140
So if you come up with a new way
of connectors, or are updated,

00:31:34.140 --> 00:31:35.940
if there was a bug
and you fixed it,

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:37.770
you should be able to
use that seamlessly.

00:31:37.770 --> 00:31:40.680
And the system allows you
to migrate from version A

00:31:40.680 --> 00:31:43.620
to version B, making
sure all of those checks

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:45.810
have been handled carefully.

00:31:45.810 --> 00:31:48.938
So with that, this is
essentially an ability

00:31:48.938 --> 00:31:50.730
to build a batch
pipeline, but you can also

00:31:50.730 --> 00:31:53.910
do the same thing with real
time pipelines, actually.

00:31:53.910 --> 00:31:56.290
So you can build a bunch
of real-time pipelines.

00:31:56.290 --> 00:31:57.900
Looks like there are a few--

00:31:57.900 --> 00:32:00.570
there are not that many
plugins available here,

00:32:00.570 --> 00:32:02.790
but that's the reason
we have added Hub.

00:32:02.790 --> 00:32:05.100
So Hub is a place
where you can--

00:32:05.100 --> 00:32:07.410
we will be adding
more connectors, more

00:32:07.410 --> 00:32:09.900
transformations, more
reusable components here,

00:32:09.900 --> 00:32:12.270
that we'll be making
it available here.

00:32:12.270 --> 00:32:15.030
And the best part
is you can have

00:32:15.030 --> 00:32:22.260
your own organizational
internal hub.

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:24.930
So you can share some of
the connectors of plugins

00:32:24.930 --> 00:32:29.190
that you're doing with the other
users within your organization.

00:32:29.190 --> 00:32:31.800
It makes it extremely
easy for you to do that.

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:34.530
It's a spec that we ask you--

00:32:34.530 --> 00:32:35.940
it's actually a documented spec.

00:32:35.940 --> 00:32:37.770
So if you can follow
that spec, you

00:32:37.770 --> 00:32:41.730
can get in exactly the same
market for your organization.

00:32:41.730 --> 00:32:44.720
In addition to
what we do provide.

00:32:44.720 --> 00:32:47.390
And here, where we put stuff,
there's a lot of things

00:32:47.390 --> 00:32:49.018
that we have built
over the years,

00:32:49.018 --> 00:32:50.310
over the period of seven years.

00:32:50.310 --> 00:32:52.760
But we're also getting
a lot of contributions

00:32:52.760 --> 00:32:55.580
from within Google, our
open source community,

00:32:55.580 --> 00:32:57.800
as well as, we are working
with a lot of partners

00:32:57.800 --> 00:32:59.780
to be able to build this.

00:32:59.780 --> 00:33:01.370
And the one
important distinction

00:33:01.370 --> 00:33:03.230
that we're making, right?

00:33:03.230 --> 00:33:08.930
When I talk about Data Fusion,
Data Fusion is actually not--

00:33:08.930 --> 00:33:09.812
it's unlimited use.

00:33:09.812 --> 00:33:11.270
At every instance
there is no limit

00:33:11.270 --> 00:33:12.990
on the number of users
who can access it.

00:33:12.990 --> 00:33:14.120
That's number one.

00:33:14.120 --> 00:33:16.310
Between basic and
enterprise we are not

00:33:16.310 --> 00:33:18.710
distinguishing connectors.

00:33:18.710 --> 00:33:23.780
Every single connector will
be available in both of them.

00:33:23.780 --> 00:33:30.020
Unlike when you look at how
we essentially do connectors,

00:33:30.020 --> 00:33:33.055
generally we didn't want
to give that choice.

00:33:33.055 --> 00:33:35.180
Essentially, we wanted to
keep it plain and simple,

00:33:35.180 --> 00:33:37.610
so every connector would
be available of the things

00:33:37.610 --> 00:33:38.800
that we built.

00:33:38.800 --> 00:33:42.410
So from here, you want DB2
plugin, you can just deploy it,

00:33:42.410 --> 00:33:44.840
it gets added to your instance.

00:33:44.840 --> 00:33:45.510
That's it.

00:33:45.510 --> 00:33:50.990
With that you are now able
to use the DB2 plugin to talk

00:33:50.990 --> 00:33:53.092
to DB2 instances.

00:33:53.092 --> 00:33:54.300
So the same thing holds true.

00:33:54.300 --> 00:33:55.550
So there are a lot of
different real time

00:33:55.550 --> 00:33:57.080
connectors that are available.

00:33:57.080 --> 00:33:58.730
Transforms get shared.

00:33:58.730 --> 00:34:02.630
Analytics of different kinds,
where you're actually doing

00:34:02.630 --> 00:34:05.840
joins or you're actually
aggregating data,

00:34:05.840 --> 00:34:06.890
you're profiling data.

00:34:06.890 --> 00:34:09.350
All that stuff is
readily available.

00:34:09.350 --> 00:34:11.540
And the real-time
pipelines actually,

00:34:11.540 --> 00:34:14.216
right now when they
run in GCP, they

00:34:14.216 --> 00:34:17.400
run as Spark
streaming pipelines.

00:34:17.400 --> 00:34:21.659
OK, with that, let me
show you a little bit of--

00:34:21.659 --> 00:34:23.620
a few things that
you can do with this.

00:34:23.620 --> 00:34:27.390
So this is a very simple
example of a pipeline that

00:34:27.390 --> 00:34:29.730
was deployed here, where
you're essentially taking data

00:34:29.730 --> 00:34:32.130
from various different
sources, joining them together,

00:34:32.130 --> 00:34:35.400
and writing it into a BigQuery.

00:34:35.400 --> 00:34:39.230
This pipeline, I think,
has been scheduled to run.

00:34:39.230 --> 00:34:42.675
You get operational
metrics on this pipeline.

00:34:42.675 --> 00:34:44.300
You're able to see
how this pipeline is

00:34:44.300 --> 00:34:46.639
behaving over a period of time.

00:34:46.639 --> 00:34:48.440
All of this data
that I'm showing you,

00:34:48.440 --> 00:34:54.409
all of the stuff that I just
did from, sorry, from the UI,

00:34:54.409 --> 00:34:56.960
you are able to do
those using REST APIs.

00:34:56.960 --> 00:35:01.820
Every single aspect of this
is available through publicly

00:35:01.820 --> 00:35:03.672
available REST APIs.

00:35:03.672 --> 00:35:05.880
So if you're looking for
something on this dashboard,

00:35:05.880 --> 00:35:07.980
it's actually available
as a REST API for you

00:35:07.980 --> 00:35:10.060
to pull so that
you can integrate

00:35:10.060 --> 00:35:12.060
with your internal systems
if you would like to.

00:35:14.640 --> 00:35:17.300
So, now, if you see
here, what's happening

00:35:17.300 --> 00:35:19.730
is, when you ran this
pipeline, actually,

00:35:19.730 --> 00:35:23.380
this pipeline is using
a Dataproc profile

00:35:23.380 --> 00:35:25.470
that is automatically
created for you.

00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:28.340
So there is this notion of
provisioners and profiles

00:35:28.340 --> 00:35:31.930
and profiles are based
off of provisioners.

00:35:31.930 --> 00:35:35.950
So here is a profile that
the system automatically

00:35:35.950 --> 00:35:37.240
creates for you.

00:35:37.240 --> 00:35:39.820
And you can see,
using this profile,

00:35:39.820 --> 00:35:42.760
all the different pipelines
that are being executed

00:35:42.760 --> 00:35:45.940
and how much time they take
from an execution perspective.

00:35:45.940 --> 00:35:47.640
So you can define
different profiles

00:35:47.640 --> 00:35:49.015
for different
kinds of workloads.

00:35:49.015 --> 00:35:51.700
You know, if you're
doing large migration,

00:35:51.700 --> 00:35:54.940
you want to be able to say
that you're doing heavy ETL

00:35:54.940 --> 00:35:55.630
transformations.

00:35:55.630 --> 00:35:58.210
You should be able to
specify a profile for that.

00:35:58.210 --> 00:36:00.850
So something that can be
controlled by administrators,

00:36:00.850 --> 00:36:03.760
but also, if needed,
that users themselves

00:36:03.760 --> 00:36:08.086
can change them if they
have the right permissions.

00:36:11.710 --> 00:36:14.710
So you have also
the ability to look

00:36:14.710 --> 00:36:16.260
at every run of the pipelines.

00:36:16.260 --> 00:36:18.210
So this is just one
execution of the pipeline.

00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:19.360
So it can go back in time.

00:36:19.360 --> 00:36:21.790
So we do keep, for
long periods of time,

00:36:21.790 --> 00:36:27.060
all of this data in Data Fusion.

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:30.120
And one important
thing, so this pipeline

00:36:30.120 --> 00:36:31.590
could be scheduled, right?

00:36:31.590 --> 00:36:33.960
This pipeline also
can be triggered

00:36:33.960 --> 00:36:37.340
by the execution actions
that are generated

00:36:37.340 --> 00:36:39.170
from the previous pipeline.

00:36:39.170 --> 00:36:43.280
Right, so I can say trigger
this run of the pipeline

00:36:43.280 --> 00:36:46.550
only when the previous pipeline
successfully completes, or it

00:36:46.550 --> 00:36:49.370
has just completed, or it
has failed, then trigger it.

00:36:49.370 --> 00:36:50.908
So you have an
ability to do that.

00:36:50.908 --> 00:36:52.700
And when you talk about
different use cases

00:36:52.700 --> 00:36:54.533
where you're looking
at many different teams

00:36:54.533 --> 00:36:56.930
involved in building a
much larger pipeline which

00:36:56.930 --> 00:36:59.810
is organization-wide,
or there's a team

00:36:59.810 --> 00:37:02.660
responsible for bringing data
in from external sources,

00:37:02.660 --> 00:37:05.640
standardizing them, landing
it in a staging area.

00:37:05.640 --> 00:37:08.690
And then from there,
analytics team picks that data

00:37:08.690 --> 00:37:11.480
and runs their analytics
and essentially

00:37:11.480 --> 00:37:13.990
do some aggregations
and write them into BQ.

00:37:13.990 --> 00:37:16.070
Another team could be
taking it from there.

00:37:16.070 --> 00:37:17.960
Now the thing is,
information needs

00:37:17.960 --> 00:37:22.110
to be passed from execution
of one pipeline to the other.

00:37:22.110 --> 00:37:25.480
So for example, where you are
landing on the staging area.

00:37:25.480 --> 00:37:29.420
So with tokens that are being
passed between these pipelines,

00:37:29.420 --> 00:37:31.400
you're able to do that.

00:37:31.400 --> 00:37:35.090
And you can pick and
choose whether which tokens

00:37:35.090 --> 00:37:36.090
you want to pass.

00:37:36.090 --> 00:37:39.120
And in this example you
have plugins you can pass,

00:37:39.120 --> 00:37:40.610
runtime arguments
you can pass, you

00:37:40.610 --> 00:37:42.430
can even pass compute
configs in there.

00:37:45.410 --> 00:37:48.920
So this is just a simple
example of where you're doing

00:37:48.920 --> 00:37:50.240
all of the different joins.

00:37:50.240 --> 00:37:53.540
There can be far more complex
pipelines you can build.

00:37:53.540 --> 00:37:56.550
So I want to show you
guys one other pipeline.

00:37:56.550 --> 00:37:59.920
So this is a real-time
pipeline which is not running,

00:37:59.920 --> 00:38:01.170
it should be running.

00:38:01.170 --> 00:38:03.500
So this is a very simple
real-time pipeline

00:38:03.500 --> 00:38:08.820
which actually is watching for
data that is arriving on GCS.

00:38:08.820 --> 00:38:12.790
As soon as the data arrives,
it passes, cleanses the data,

00:38:12.790 --> 00:38:14.660
sends it to data profiling.

00:38:14.660 --> 00:38:17.270
It profiles that data
for that window whatever

00:38:17.270 --> 00:38:18.410
you have specified, right?

00:38:18.410 --> 00:38:22.130
Like, if you specify a 15
minute or an hour data that's

00:38:22.130 --> 00:38:24.500
coming in, profile that,
and that profile information

00:38:24.500 --> 00:38:27.320
is actually returned to Pub/Sub.

00:38:27.320 --> 00:38:32.180
Now all the good data
gets returned to BigQuery

00:38:32.180 --> 00:38:34.310
and any of the errors
that are generated

00:38:34.310 --> 00:38:37.550
in the records because
of any bad data

00:38:37.550 --> 00:38:41.030
actually get separated out
and gets written into GCS.

00:38:41.030 --> 00:38:43.970
So this is a real-time
program and, of course,

00:38:43.970 --> 00:38:45.650
it's a microbatch.

00:38:45.650 --> 00:38:48.890
But when we do integrate
this with Dataflow,

00:38:48.890 --> 00:38:51.480
you will have true
streaming capability

00:38:51.480 --> 00:38:53.730
and you'll still be able
to do this kind of stuff.

00:38:59.320 --> 00:39:02.740
So very simple things.

00:39:02.740 --> 00:39:06.320
This is how we are taking
it to the next level.

00:39:06.320 --> 00:39:09.850
So this is an
example of a use case

00:39:09.850 --> 00:39:12.100
where you want to
move the data, but you

00:39:12.100 --> 00:39:14.680
have no idea of the risk
profile of that data.

00:39:14.680 --> 00:39:16.440
And you want to be
able to make sure

00:39:16.440 --> 00:39:18.520
that if there is
any sensitive data,

00:39:18.520 --> 00:39:21.160
you want to exclude that
out before you send it to,

00:39:21.160 --> 00:39:24.640
let's say, your final
data store, right?

00:39:24.640 --> 00:39:27.220
So this is something
that we are going

00:39:27.220 --> 00:39:30.910
to be making it
available soon in Q2,

00:39:30.910 --> 00:39:32.890
which is integration with DLP.

00:39:32.890 --> 00:39:38.435
So if I want to integrate with
DLP, let's just drag the DLP

00:39:38.435 --> 00:39:42.030
and put it in the configurer,
your DLP work is done, right?

00:39:42.030 --> 00:39:44.190
Now, you might
say, oh, how do we

00:39:44.190 --> 00:39:47.880
ensure that every data set
that I have needs to always

00:39:47.880 --> 00:39:50.040
be passed through DLP?

00:39:50.040 --> 00:39:53.220
So think of those two nodes and
you are essentially creating

00:39:53.220 --> 00:39:54.630
different kinds of data sets.

00:39:54.630 --> 00:39:56.190
Like, for example,
this is a GCS one

00:39:56.190 --> 00:40:00.360
that is being sent
to, let's say, DLP.

00:40:00.360 --> 00:40:04.200
So you will have the
ability to create templates.

00:40:04.200 --> 00:40:08.610
You will take GCS
and you will take DLP

00:40:08.610 --> 00:40:10.330
and you create a
template out of that.

00:40:10.330 --> 00:40:13.930
And what that will allow you to
do is essentially be able to--

00:40:13.930 --> 00:40:17.970
anytime anyone who uses that
it will always be applying DLP

00:40:17.970 --> 00:40:20.310
transformations on
that or DLP filtering

00:40:20.310 --> 00:40:21.480
or tokenizations on that.

00:40:21.480 --> 00:40:24.510
So that is much more
an easier way for you

00:40:24.510 --> 00:40:25.600
to [INAUDIBLE] things.

00:40:25.600 --> 00:40:28.700
This is a very filtered example.

00:40:28.700 --> 00:40:31.590
The filter example is
actually allowing you

00:40:31.590 --> 00:40:34.500
to only filter data, but we'll
be adding more capabilities

00:40:34.500 --> 00:40:37.750
in terms of being able to
tokenize fields and such.

00:40:37.750 --> 00:40:39.600
So you will have ability to--

00:40:39.600 --> 00:40:42.550
right now you say, with
some confidence interval,

00:40:42.550 --> 00:40:45.305
which is like if a
filter confidence is low,

00:40:45.305 --> 00:40:46.680
anytime when there
is credit card

00:40:46.680 --> 00:40:51.510
numbers that gets identified as
sensitive data, filter it out.

00:40:51.510 --> 00:40:53.940
So I can pick and choose a
bunch of different things.

00:40:53.940 --> 00:40:57.240
I will soon be able to also
tokenize them and encrypt them,

00:40:57.240 --> 00:41:00.180
a bunch of things like that.

00:41:00.180 --> 00:41:02.040
So this is all great.

00:41:02.040 --> 00:41:04.080
So this is all about
building the pipelines.

00:41:04.080 --> 00:41:06.893
I showed you guys how you
can operationalize them,

00:41:06.893 --> 00:41:08.560
what are the metrics
that are available.

00:41:08.560 --> 00:41:12.140
Logs are also similarly
available in the same fashion.

00:41:12.140 --> 00:41:14.640
But the most important thing
from an operational perspective

00:41:14.640 --> 00:41:18.740
is, can we actually monitor all
of the pipelines in one place?

00:41:18.740 --> 00:41:21.110
So you have an
ability to monitor,

00:41:21.110 --> 00:41:24.950
and there are more things
that would be enabled soon.

00:41:24.950 --> 00:41:26.990
This is actually looking
at all of the pipelines

00:41:26.990 --> 00:41:29.000
that are running
on an hour by hour

00:41:29.000 --> 00:41:32.178
and you will be able to look
at each individual pipeline

00:41:32.178 --> 00:41:34.220
and how they were being
executed, what was there,

00:41:34.220 --> 00:41:36.290
like what time did it take?

00:41:36.290 --> 00:41:39.950
In fact, we also include
things like start delays.

00:41:39.950 --> 00:41:41.720
So what ends up
happening is, we have

00:41:41.720 --> 00:41:44.330
a lot of customers who
schedule the pipeline,

00:41:44.330 --> 00:41:48.470
actually, to be running at like,
let's say, at 2 in the morning.

00:41:48.470 --> 00:41:52.340
Because it happens all
independent of each other.

00:41:52.340 --> 00:41:55.590
The capacity within that project
gets hit because everything

00:41:55.590 --> 00:41:57.828
starts around the same time.

00:41:57.828 --> 00:41:58.870
So how do you avoid that?

00:41:58.870 --> 00:42:02.440
So this has a little bit
of a futuristic view, one.

00:42:02.440 --> 00:42:05.453
Two, it also gives you
an idea of how much time

00:42:05.453 --> 00:42:06.870
it is taking to
start something up

00:42:06.870 --> 00:42:09.210
because the resources
are not available.

00:42:09.210 --> 00:42:12.120
So you can actually
go into future

00:42:12.120 --> 00:42:15.210
and look at what are the
different jobs that are

00:42:15.210 --> 00:42:17.260
being scheduled at every hour.

00:42:17.260 --> 00:42:19.740
And what does that mean
from a capacity perspective

00:42:19.740 --> 00:42:22.110
to be able to run
that in that hour.

00:42:22.110 --> 00:42:24.210
So you get a visibility
into those kind

00:42:24.210 --> 00:42:26.220
of things, which
makes it extremely

00:42:26.220 --> 00:42:30.140
easy from an
operations perspective.

00:42:30.140 --> 00:42:34.750
So one last thing before
we go into Q&amp;A session

00:42:34.750 --> 00:42:39.130
is the metadata that
we talked about.

00:42:39.130 --> 00:42:41.020
So I can search--

00:42:41.020 --> 00:42:43.480
this is all in the context
of integration, right?

00:42:43.480 --> 00:42:45.250
So this is all in
context of integration

00:42:45.250 --> 00:42:47.650
and I know you guys will
have lots of questions

00:42:47.650 --> 00:42:50.450
around how does this
integrate with Data Catalog?

00:42:50.450 --> 00:42:53.620
So let me give you an
answer before you ask.

00:42:53.620 --> 00:42:57.400
But the whole idea here is
we are both at a beta product

00:42:57.400 --> 00:42:59.020
right now, but at
some point, we will

00:42:59.020 --> 00:43:01.420
be making a read-only
version of all

00:43:01.420 --> 00:43:04.250
of this metadata available
in Data Catalog, number one.

00:43:04.250 --> 00:43:07.930
Number two, we'll still continue
enhancing the experiences

00:43:07.930 --> 00:43:10.630
of solving the problems
from a data integration

00:43:10.630 --> 00:43:12.520
perspective in Data Fusion.

00:43:12.520 --> 00:43:14.230
So that will still remain here.

00:43:14.230 --> 00:43:16.560
So things like deep lineage,
field level lineage,

00:43:16.560 --> 00:43:20.080
a bunch of things that are very,
very relevant to integration

00:43:20.080 --> 00:43:22.800
would still be
available in here.

00:43:22.800 --> 00:43:24.550
So this is the data
set that I was looking

00:43:24.550 --> 00:43:30.290
at that was the joint pipeline
and I can see the schema here,

00:43:30.290 --> 00:43:32.270
we can attach business tags.

00:43:32.270 --> 00:43:34.120
So this is like, I
don't know, I'm just

00:43:34.120 --> 00:43:35.652
going to say doubleclick here.

00:43:35.652 --> 00:43:37.360
So I should be able
to immediately search

00:43:37.360 --> 00:43:38.470
based on that.

00:43:38.470 --> 00:43:41.200
You can look at lineage
at a data set level,

00:43:41.200 --> 00:43:43.750
but the interesting thing
is, you can look at lineage

00:43:43.750 --> 00:43:45.970
at the individual field level.

00:43:45.970 --> 00:43:48.460
So you are able to
actually figure out

00:43:48.460 --> 00:43:50.620
what happens to
individual fields

00:43:50.620 --> 00:43:52.540
when the data is being ingested.

00:43:52.540 --> 00:43:55.720
So, for example, if I'm
looking at a landing page URL,

00:43:55.720 --> 00:43:58.240
this actually comes from
a landing page data set.

00:43:58.240 --> 00:44:00.710
If I'm looking for
a referrer URL,

00:44:00.710 --> 00:44:03.070
it's coming from
impression data set.

00:44:03.070 --> 00:44:04.720
If I'm looking at
advertiser field,

00:44:04.720 --> 00:44:06.970
it's coming from an
advertiser data set.

00:44:06.970 --> 00:44:08.770
But the interesting
thing is, what

00:44:08.770 --> 00:44:10.510
are the operations
that are being applied

00:44:10.510 --> 00:44:13.080
as the data was being moved?

00:44:13.080 --> 00:44:15.960
It went through different joins,
it was read from the source.

00:44:15.960 --> 00:44:19.090
So our goal is to be able
to write this in a normal,

00:44:19.090 --> 00:44:21.580
like an English text,
so that you can just

00:44:21.580 --> 00:44:24.970
read this to be able to see
how your data is actually

00:44:24.970 --> 00:44:26.380
going to be moving.

00:44:26.380 --> 00:44:29.035
That is the goal
and we basically

00:44:29.035 --> 00:44:31.660
are starting to do that,
but there's a long way

00:44:31.660 --> 00:44:34.112
to go in terms of making
it full English text

00:44:34.112 --> 00:44:35.320
so that you can just read it.

00:44:35.320 --> 00:44:38.690
But you'll get an idea of
where we are going with this.

00:44:38.690 --> 00:44:42.230
So just being able to look at
that the data has come from

00:44:42.230 --> 00:44:45.340
and where it is going to go
gives you a lot of flexibility

00:44:45.340 --> 00:44:46.150
to do that.

00:44:46.150 --> 00:44:50.100
[MUSIC PLAYING]

